{"ratings":[{"id":"C1","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"plausible","reason":"After a reset and restart, a resumed run can silently use a model removed from its routing cell."},{"id":"C2","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"plausible","reason":"A prior routing edit causes later escalations to be discarded on the next round."},{"id":"C3","consequence":"outage","trigger":"plausible","reason":"A persisted override naming a retired model or removed provider can prevent daemon startup and cannot be reset through the CLI while the daemon is down."},{"id":"C4","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"plausible","reason":"An unrelated routing edit can reorder contended provider queues and create misleading cancellation records."},{"id":"C5","consequence":"diag","trigger":"plausible","reason":"Invalid routing input produces a hard-to-read validator dump while retaining the reason for rejection."},{"id":"C7","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"plausible","reason":"The eval policy diff can report an effective routing change that writing the proposed overlay will not make."},{"id":"C8","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"plausible","reason":"Resetting an absent override can unnecessarily interrupt and reorder queued invocations as well as add spurious history entries."},{"id":"C9","consequence":"diag","trigger":"normal","reason":"The documentation's page summary omits provider cards also shown on Models, although its statement about the Providers page is true."}]}